A Vector DB doesn’t “look” like a normal table. Instead, it stores embeddings + metadata.

Let me show you exactly how it looks in practice.

🧠 1. Conceptual View (Simple)

A Vector DB record looks like this:

ID: "doc_1"

Vector (embedding):
[0.12, -0.45, 0.88, 0.33, ...]

Metadata:
{
  "text": "I love dogs",
  "category": "animals"
}


👉 So instead of rows & columns, it’s:

ID
Vector (numbers)
Metadata (optional info)
📦 2. Real Example (Like Chroma / Pinecone)

Imagine you store 3 sentences:

Data you insert:
"I love dogs"
"Cats are amazing"
"I enjoy coding"

After embedding (what DB actually stores):
[
  {
    id: "1",
    embedding: [0.21, -0.34, 0.78, ...],
    metadata: { text: "I love dogs" }
  },
  {
    id: "2",
    embedding: [0.11, -0.90, 0.65, ...],
    metadata: { text: "Cats are amazing" }
  },
  {
    id: "3",
    embedding: [0.77, 0.12, -0.44, ...],
    metadata: { text: "I enjoy coding" }
  }
]

🔍 3. How Query Looks Internally
You ask:
"Tell me about pets"

Step 1: Convert to vector
[0.20, -0.30, 0.75, ...]

Step 2: DB compares with stored vectors

It calculates similarity:

Query vs "I love dogs"      → 0.95 ✅
Query vs "Cats are amazing" → 0.91 ✅
Query vs "I enjoy coding"   → 0.10 ❌

Step 3: Result returned
[
  "I love dogs",
  "Cats are amazing"
]


👉 This is called nearest neighbor search

⚙️ 4. Example in Code (Python with Chroma)
collection.add(
    ids=["1", "2"],
    documents=["I love dogs", "Cats are amazing"]
)


Behind the scenes, it becomes:

id → vector → metadata

Query:
collection.query(
    query_texts=["pets"],
    n_results=2
)

Output:
{
  "documents": [
    ["I love dogs", "Cats are amazing"]
  ]
}

📊 5. Compare with SQL Table
Traditional DB:
id	text
1	I love dogs
2	Cats are amazing
Vector DB (mental model):
id	vector	metadata
1	[0.21, -0.34, ...]	"I love dogs"
2	[0.11, -0.90, ...]	"Cats are amazing"
🧠 6. Important Insight

👉 You never manually create vectors

They are generated using models like:

OpenAI embeddings
Sentence Transformers
🚀 7. Real-Life Use (RAG)

In apps like ChatGPT:

Your documents → converted to vectors → stored
Your question → converted to vector
Vector DB → finds similar docs
LLM → uses those docs to answer